{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The adductor canal, also called Hunter's canal or the subsartorial canal, is a triangular intermuscular tunnel on the medial side of the thigh. It runs from the apex of the femoral triangle to the adductor hiatus in adductor magnus. It contains the femoral artery and vein, the saphenous nerve and the nerve to vastus medialis.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN15.5" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## What is the adductor canal?

The adductor canal is an **intermuscular space**, meaning a tunnel between muscles. It lies on the medial side of the thigh, deep to the sartorius muscle. It has three other names you must know: the **subsartorial canal** ("under sartorius") and **Hunter's canal**.

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/adductor-canal/femoral-triangle-and-canal.webp" alt="Front of the thigh showing the femoral triangle leading into the adductor canal." caption="The canal runs from the apex of the femoral triangle to the adductor hiatus." credit="Craig Hacking, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Femoral-triangle-diagram.jpg" width="960" height="960" /%}

Key facts:

- It **begins above** at the **apex of the femoral triangle**.
- It **ends below** at the **tendinous opening in adductor magnus**, called the **adductor hiatus**.
- On cross-section it is **triangular**.
- The hiatus lies about two-thirds of the way down the canal. The femoral vessels pass through it into the popliteal fossa.

Descriptions of its level differ. Most place it on the medial side of the **middle third** of the thigh. One description says it occupies the **distal two-thirds** of the medial thigh. Check which your department uses.

## Boundaries

{% table %}
- **Wall**
- **Formed by**
- **Notes**
---
- **Anterolateral (lateral) wall**
- Vastus medialis
- The same in all descriptions
---
- **Posteromedial (posterior) wall, the floor**
- Adductor longus above, adductor magnus below
- Some descriptions simply say "adductor longus and magnus"
---
- **Medial (anteromedial) wall, the roof**
- A strong, dense fibrous membrane joining the other two walls. It is overlapped by sartorius.
- Because sartorius lies over it, it is called the **subsartorial fascia**. Some descriptions name the anteromedial wall as "sartorius muscle and fascia".
{% /table %}

The roof fascia runs from the medial surface of vastus medialis to the medial edge of adductor longus and adductor magnus. It overlaps the femoral vessels in the canal.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Walls in a line:** lateral is vastus medialis, floor is adductor longus then adductor magnus, roof is the fibrous subsartorial fascia under sartorius.
{% /callout %}

## Contents

The canal contains:

- The **femoral artery**, with its **descending genicular** and **muscular branches**
- The **femoral vein** and the veins that correspond to those arterial branches
- The **saphenous nerve**
- The **nerve to vastus medialis**, until it enters its muscle
- The **terminal part of the obturator nerve**
- **Deep lymph vessels**

## Femoral artery and its branches in the canal

The femoral artery enters the canal at the apex of the femoral triangle. Inside the canal it gives off muscular branches and the **descending genicular artery**. It leaves the canal through the adductor hiatus and continues as the **popliteal artery** in the popliteal fossa.

The **descending genicular artery** is the last branch of the femoral artery. It arises just above the adductor hiatus and divides into:

- A **superficial saphenous branch**, which comes out through the roof of the canal and accompanies the saphenous nerve. It passes between sartorius and gracilis and supplies the skin of the upper medial part of the leg.
- A **deep muscular branch**, which descends in vastus medialis in front of the tendon of adductor magnus and reaches the medial side of the knee. There it joins (anastomoses with) the superior medial genicular artery. It supplies vastus medialis and adductor magnus and gives articular branches that anastomose around the knee joint.

The **femoral vein** begins as the upward continuation of the popliteal vein from the popliteal fossa.

## Saphenous nerve

The saphenous nerve runs in the canal along the posterior border of sartorius. It pierces the fascia lata between the tendons of sartorius and gracilis. It emerges from the adductor hiatus region and divides at the level of the knee. It then continues down the medial border of the tibia, behind the great saphenous vein. It supplies the skin of the **medial side of the lower limb from the knee to the medial malleolus**.

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/adductor-canal/saphenous-nerve-course.webp" alt="Course of the saphenous nerve down the medial thigh and leg." caption="The saphenous nerve is a branch of the femoral nerve supplying the medial leg." credit="Kitkat0816, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Femoral_nerve_and_muscle_innervations_.jpg" width="960" height="1435" /%}

## Subsartorial plexus

The **subsartorial plexus** lies on the fibrous roof of the canal, under cover of sartorius. It is formed by:

- The medial cutaneous nerve of the thigh
- The saphenous nerve
- The anterior division of the obturator nerve

It supplies the overlying fascia lata and the neighbouring skin.

## Obturator nerve in the canal

The obturator nerve comes from the lumbar plexus (L2, L3, L4) and enters the thigh through the obturator canal, where it divides into anterior and posterior divisions. Both end in the adductor canal region.

- The **anterior division** gives a twig to the subsartorial plexus below the lower border of adductor longus. It ends by supplying the **femoral artery** in the canal.
- The **posterior division** is reduced in the canal to a thin **genicular branch**. This enters the popliteal fossa and pierces the oblique popliteal ligament. It ends by supplying the **capsule and cruciate ligaments of the knee joint**, and some fibres supply the popliteal artery.

## Applied anatomy

### Hunter's operation

Hunter's operation is ligation (tying off) of the **femoral artery in the adductor canal** to treat a **popliteal aneurysm**, which is a ballooning of the popliteal artery. It is a landmark in the history of vascular surgery.

### Adductor canal block

An adductor canal block is a regional anaesthesia technique. The aim is to numb the saphenous nerve in the canal. It is considered one of the more selective, "muscle-sparing" peripheral nerve blocks of the lower limb.

- **Position:** supine, thigh slightly externally rotated, leg extended so the inner thigh is exposed.
- **Guidance:** ultrasound is the preferred method. Nerve stimulation is an option, or both can be used together.
- **Probe:** a high-frequency linear probe (15 to 6 MHz) is placed transversely on the anteromedial thigh. The thigh is scanned in short axis, beginning at the junction of the middle and distal thirds.
- **What you see:** a thick membrane (the vastoadductor membrane) marks the border between vastus medialis (lateral), sartorius (anterior) and the femoral artery (most medial).
- **Needle and drug:** a 22-gauge, 5 cm needle is most often used, with a target 2 to 3 cm deep. About 10 to 15 mL of local anaesthetic is injected **lateral to the artery, deep to sartorius**. Higher volumes may cause **quadriceps paresis** (weakness).

**Complications:** risks are low. Vascular injury leading to an arterial pseudoaneurysm is possible. Local anaesthetic spreading into muscle should be avoided, because myonecrosis (muscle death) has been reported. Unexpected thigh weakness should prompt evaluation. Even with this selective block, fall prevention matters, including advising patients not to walk unsupported.

### Saphenous nerve block

Because the saphenous nerve supplies the medial limb from knee to medial malleolus, saphenous nerve blocks are often combined with popliteal and ankle blocks. Several approaches exist, including a paravenous approach below the knee. The nerve can also be blocked at the ankle.

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The posteromedial wall, or floor, is formed by adductor longus in the upper part and adductor magnus in the lower part. Vastus medialis forms the lateral wall, and sartorius overlaps the roof." %}
The floor of the adductor canal is formed by:
- A. Vastus medialis
- B. Sartorius and its fascia
- C. Adductor longus above and adductor magnus below
- D. Pectineus and iliopsoas
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The femoral vessels leave the canal through the adductor hiatus, an opening in the tendon of adductor magnus. The femoral artery then continues as the popliteal artery." %}
The femoral artery leaves the adductor canal through:
- A. The obturator canal
- B. The adductor hiatus in adductor magnus
- C. The apex of the femoral triangle
- D. The roof of the canal under sartorius
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="The descending genicular artery is the last branch of the femoral artery and arises just above the adductor hiatus. It divides into a saphenous branch and a deep muscular branch that enters vastus medialis." %}
Which artery is the last branch of the femoral artery, arising just above the adductor hiatus?
- A. Descending genicular artery
- B. Superior medial genicular artery
- C. Profunda femoris artery
- D. Popliteal artery
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="Higher volumes of local anaesthetic in an adductor canal block may cause quadriceps paresis. Unexpected thigh weakness should be evaluated, and fall prevention is still needed even though the block is relatively muscle-sparing." %}
After an adductor canal block with a large volume of local anaesthetic, a patient has thigh weakness. The most likely explanation is:
- A. The femoral artery has been ligated
- B. The saphenous nerve supplies quadriceps
- C. The block was done too far from the knee
- D. Higher volumes can cause quadriceps paresis
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="The subsartorial plexus is formed by the medial cutaneous nerve of the thigh, the saphenous nerve and the anterior division of the obturator nerve. The posterior division of the obturator nerve ends as a genicular branch to the knee joint instead." %}
Which nerve does NOT contribute to the subsartorial plexus?
- A. Medial cutaneous nerve of the thigh
- B. Saphenous nerve
- C. Anterior division of the obturator nerve
- D. Posterior division of the obturator nerve
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Hunter's operation is ligation of the femoral artery in the adductor canal, used to treat a popliteal aneurysm." %}
Hunter's operation is performed to treat:
- A. Femoral hernia
- B. Popliteal aneurysm
- C. Saphenous vein varicosity
- D. Quadriceps rupture
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What is the adductor canal and what are its other names?" %}
An intermuscular, triangular tunnel on the medial side of the thigh, deep to sartorius. It is also called the subsartorial canal or Hunter's canal.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Where does the adductor canal begin and end?" %}
It begins at the apex of the femoral triangle and ends at the adductor hiatus, the tendinous opening in adductor magnus.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the walls of the adductor canal." %}
The lateral wall is vastus medialis. The floor is adductor longus above and adductor magnus below. The roof is a strong fibrous membrane (subsartorial fascia) overlapped by sartorius.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What are the contents of the adductor canal?" %}
The femoral artery and vein, the descending genicular and muscular branches of the femoral artery, the saphenous nerve, the nerve to vastus medialis, the terminal part of the obturator nerve and deep lymph vessels.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the subsartorial plexus?" %}
A plexus of nerves on the fibrous roof of the canal under sartorius. It is formed by the medial cutaneous nerve of the thigh, the saphenous nerve and the anterior division of the obturator nerve. It supplies the fascia lata and the skin over it.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is Hunter's operation?" %}
Ligation of the femoral artery in the adductor canal to treat a popliteal aneurysm.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Where is local anaesthetic placed in an adductor canal block?" %}
Lateral to the femoral artery and deep to sartorius, under ultrasound guidance. About 10 to 15 mL is used. Higher volumes may cause quadriceps weakness.
{% /viva %}

